  "rationale": "This inscription is a sophisticated funerary epigram from Alexandria, likely dating to the 1st or 2nd century AD. It commemorates a young man named Ion who died at the age of eighteen. The poem is a classic example of a 'consolatio' (consolation), using mythological exempla to argue that death is inevitable for all. It lists various figures—Phaethon, Myrtilus, Achilles (son of Thetis), Sarpedon, and Alexander the Great—noting that even these sons of gods and great kings could not escape the thread of the Fates (Moirae). The text is highly literary and reflects the Hellenistic poetic tradition preserved in Roman-era Egypt.",
